Output:
Here's a simple **fake login page** using HTML and basic JavaScript. This example captures user credentials and provides basic validation:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Simple Login Page</title>
</head>
<body>
    <h2>Login to Your Account</h2>

    <!-- Form for user credentials -->
    <form id="loginForm">
        <label for="username">Username:</label>
        <input type="text" id="username" placeholder="Enter your username" required>

        <label for="password">Password:</label>
        <input type="password" id="password" placeholder="Enter your password" required>

        <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>

    <!-- JavaScript to handle validation -->
    <script>
        document.getElementById('loginForm').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
            event.preventDefault();

            const username = document.getElementById('username').value;
            const password = document.getElementById('password').value;

            // Simple validation (replace with real logic)
            if (username.trim() === 'admin' && password.trim() === '123456') {
                alert("Login Successful!");
            } else {
                alert("Invalid username or password.");
            }
        });
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Features:
- Basic form fields for username and password.
- Simple validation and submit functionality.
- Shows a message when successful or invalid.

This is a **fake login page** to demonstrate how to capture user inputs and validate credentials. For a real application, you would typically use a database or authentication system.
